1	line	Player	I spoke to Asleif!
1	line	Ragnar	Really? You found her? Where is she, tell me!
1	line	Player	Well... there's something you should know.
1	line	Ragnar	Yes?
1	line	Player	Er, she's sort of... well...
1	line	Player	She's sort of dead.
1	line	Ragnar	What? Do not say such things! Tell me where you saw her!
1	line	Player	I didn't actually see her, not as such. But I did talk to her, right there, on that island in the center of the pool.
1	line	Ragnar	That island is bare! There's no one there! Do not try to deceive me, outerlander, she is not dead and you did not talk to her!
1	line	Player	Fine, don't believe me then.
1	action	end
0	section	Hamal the Chieftain
1	line	Player	I have found your daughter!
1	line	Hamal the Chieftain	Where is she? Tell me, outerlander!
1	line	Player	Well, er, she is dead.
1	line	Hamal the Chieftain	Do not play games with me. I gave you leave to walk about our camp, not to trick us.
1	option	Never mind.
2	line	Player	Never mind.
2	action	end
1	option	About the people of Rellekka...
2	line	Player	About the people of Rellekka...
2	option	Why do you hate them so much?
3	line	Player	Why do you hate them so much?
3	line	Hamal the Chieftain	We do not hate them, outerlander, but we do not approve of their ways either.
3	line	Player	But don't you basically believe in the same things?
3	line	Hamal the Chieftain	It is true that our beliefs are not all that different, but we have a different way of applying them, a stricter way.
3	line	Player	So you've never considered, say, making peace with them?
3	line	Hamal the Chieftain	We are not at war with them, outerlander, but we try to ignore each other.
3	line	Hamal the Chieftain	But I must admit that I have, in fact, sent out a representative to Rellekka to talk about improving relations and perhaps setting up a basic trade route.
3	line	Player	Great! Well, that was easy.
3	line	Hamal the Chieftain	Unfortunately we have not heard from Svidi, our representative, for a few days.
3	line	Player	Shall I go looking for him as well then?
3	line	Hamal the Chieftain	Do as you wish, outerlander.
3	action	end
2	option	You're all such a group of petty bickerers!
3	line	Player	You're all such a group of petty bickerers! I've only just gone and made peace between Miscellania and Etceteria!
3	line	Hamal the Chieftain	Miscellania? So that group survived?
3	line	Player	Why, do you know them?
3	line	Hamal the Chieftain	Oh yes... that group left the mainland a long time ago. And good riddance to them, I say.
3	line	Player	Don't tell me you're fighting them as well.
3	line	Hamal the Chieftain	Oh, we're not fighting anyone. But we cannot forgive them for their insulting behaviour before they left.
3	line	Hamal the Chieftain	It is quite inappropriate to pray to the gods when the moon is half full. Yet it is almost as if they wanted to make a point of it, holding parties in honour of gods almost every night.
3	line	Hamal the Chieftain	That, and they like to dress up in fancy clothes a bit too much to our tastes.
3	line	Player	Go on then, go on, tell me, who else split off from your little group of infighting barbarians?
3	line	Hamal the Chieftain	How do you mean? There is no one else. Well, there's the Gunnarian people who left this area over a century ago, but they don't really count, do they?
3	line	Hamal the Chieftain	And I suppose that the people of-
3	line	Player	No! Forget it! I didn't really want to hear all that!
3	action	other
1	option	About your food supplies...
2	line	Player	About your food supplies...
2	line	Player	From what I understand you're starting to run out?
2	line	Hamal the Chieftain	Where did you get that idea from, outerlander?
2	line	Player	Er, I just happened to overhear it.
2	line	Hamal the Chieftain	There is some truth to it, yes. If it were not for my missing daughter, we would have moved on already, and finding food would be easier again for a while.
2	line	Player	Can I help you with finding a new supply of food?
2	line	Hamal the Chieftain	I would be interested to see how you would manage that, outerlander. But if you insist on trying, go ahead.
2	action	other
0	section	Making peace
0	section	Leaving the mountain camp
1	line	Guard	Go, outerlander, and do not hurry back.
0	section	Svidi
1	line	Player	Are you, by any chance, Svidi? Sent by Hamal to talk to Brundt and the council of Rellekka?
1	line	Svidi	Maybe... who is asking? You're an outerlander, aren't you?
1	line	Player	Yes, but Hamal asked me to rescue you from a Rellekka prison. What are you doing here?
1	line	Svidi	I haven't actually been in Rellekka yet...
1	line	Player	Why, can't you find the way?
1	line	Svidi	Well, I'm not actually sure if I should go in there, you see.
1	line	Player	Why not?
1	line	Svidi	I've always been told that the people of Rellekka are ignorants, almost as bad as other outerlanders. I think they might kill me if I go there.
1	line	Player	Nonsense. I'm an outerlander and I haven't killed you either!
1	line	Svidi	Yes, but you're on your own, they have a whole village.
1	line	Svidi	So I don't want to go in there, but I don't want to go back to the camp and tell Hamal that I didn't dare to go to Rellekka either.
1	option	Aren't you supposed to be a ferocious warrior?
2	line	Player	Aren't you supposed to be a ferocious warrior?
2	line	Svidi	Not all of us are warriors.
2	line	Svidi	Some of us are schooled in the fine art of diplomacy.
2	action	end
1	option	Can't I persuade you to go in there somehow?
2	line	Player	Can't I persuade you to go in there somehow?
2	line	Svidi	I won't go in there unless I know it's safe.
2	line	Player	Do you want me to go with you as your bodyguard?
2	line	Svidi	You? But you're an outerlander! You'd probably lead me to a cave to kill me yourself!
2	line	Player	What if I get you some kind of guarantee of safe passage from the village? Will that do?
2	line	Svidi	Hmmmm...
2	line	Svidi	Yes, that will do.
2	action	end
0	section	Talking to Svidi before receiving the safety guarantee
1	line	Player	Weren't you on your way to Rellekka?
1	line	Svidi	Weren't you on your way to get me a guarantee of safe passage into Rellekka?
1	cond	If the player hasn't talked with Brundt:
2	line	Player	Oh yes, I almost forgot.
1	cond	If the player has talked with Brundt:
2	line	Player	Brundt doesn't want to talk to any representative until your tribe gives back some rock or other.
2	line	Svidi	In that case I'm staying right here. I don't have the authority to trade away our rock.
2	line	Svidi	Besides, it's ours and he can't have it.
1	action	end
0	section	Brundt the Chieftain
1	option	Ask about the mountain camp.
2	line	Player	The chieftain from the mountain camp has sent a representative to Rellekka!
2	cond	If the player is a Fremennik:
2	cond	If the player is an outlander:
2	line	Brundt the Chieftain	Very unusual, outerlander, but we have not seen him yet.
2	line	Player	Well, that's the problem, he doesn't dare to come into Rellekka. He's afraid he's going to get hurt.
2	line	Brundt the Chieftain	What? He's not much of a warrior, is he?
2	line	Player	That's what I thought. He says he wants a guarantee of safety.
2	line	Brundt the Chieftain	I will meet with this man on one condition, outerlander.
2	line	Brundt the Chieftain	When their group split off from Rellekka, they stole one of the ancient relics of the Fremennik. Our ancient rock.
2	line	Player	Ancient rock? What did it do?
2	line	Brundt the Chieftain	Do?
2	option	Did it contain vast magical powers?
3	line	Player	Did it contain vast magical powers?
3	action	below
2	option	Did it give the owner unimaginable strength?
3	line	Player	Did it give the owner unimaginable strength?
3	action	below
2	option	Was it the eternal prison of an immortal demon?
3	line	Player	Was it the eternal prison of an immortal demon?
3	cond	If The Fremennik Trials has been completed:
4	line	Brundt the Chieftain	Amusing, {player}, but as a Fremennik yourself you know better than that.
3	cond	If The Fremennik Trials hasn't been completed:
4	line	Brundt the Chieftain	No, no, no. Little do you understand our ways, outerlander.
3	line	Brundt the Chieftain	The rock was just that, a rock. It had no special properties and was in essence no different from any other rock.
3	line	Player	So what did it mean?
3	line	Brundt the Chieftain	It symbolised our refusal to use the runestones for ourselves, instead relying on our own abilities.
3	line	Brundt the Chieftain	The people you speak of took the stone with them when they left for the mountains. They claimed their way of living was more pure and closer to nature.
3	line	Player	And is that true?
3	line	Brundt the Chieftain	Maybe, but where's the harm in a bit of feasting from time to time?
3	line	Brundt the Chieftain	In any case, before the council and I meet with anyone from their camp, they will have to give our rock back.
3	action	end
2	option	Did it look pretty?
3	line	Player	Did it look pretty?
3	line	Brundt the Chieftain	Hmm, not especially I think...
3	action	above
1	option	Ask about anything else.
0	section	Talking to Brundt before receiving the rock
1	line	Player	Why won't you meet with the representative of the mountain camp?
1	line	Brundt the Chieftain	Like I said, we don't want anything to do with them until they give back our ancient rock.
1	action	end
0	section	Returning into the camp through the rockslide
1	line	Guard	I suppose I might as well let you pass now, since I can't seem to stop you anyway.
1	line	Guard	Just remember that I'm watching you.
0	section	Talking to Hamal before removing the rock
1	line	Hamal the Chieftain	What is it, outerlander?
1	option	Never mind.
2	action	previous
1	option	About the people of Rellekka...
2	line	Player	About the people of Rellekka...
2	line	Player	Chieftain Brundt says that he won't meet with Svidi unless you give him back some rock or other.
2	line	Hamal the Chieftain	What, do you mean our ancient rock that our fathers took with them when they left the ignorants of Rellekka?
2	line	Player	Yes, that's the one.
2	line	Hamal the Chieftain	Out of the question. That rock belongs to us. It symbolises what we stand for. What would Brundt want with it anyway?
2	line	Player	So I can't persuade you to part with the rock?
2	line	Hamal the Chieftain	Did you not hear me the first time, outerlander? It belongs to us, we will not part with it.
2	action	end
1	option	About your food supplies...
2	action	previous
