1	line	Player	I found him! I found your murderer! It was some bear-like man who called himself the Kendal.
1	box	'The Kendal is an ancient spirit of our people, but he is benevolent. Are you sure of this?'
1	line	Player	Well, it was actually a guy in a bearsuit. But he's dead now.
1	box	'Go then, to my father, and show him what you've learned.'
1	action	end
0	section	Talking to Hamal about the Kendal for the first time
1	line	Player	Do you know there is a strange bear like thing in a cave near the pool?
1	line	Hamal the Chieftain	Yes, it is the Kendal.
1	line	Player	Only the cave is hidden behind some trees, you see-
1	line	Player	What, you mean you know that already?
1	line	Hamal the Chieftain	Of course. We planted the trees there ourselves many years ago.
1	line	Hamal the Chieftain	It is unwise to come too close to a god, so we made sure no one would go there anymore.
1	option	But he's not a god!
2	action	continues
1	option	But he killed your daughter!
2	action	continues
1	option	And why didn't you tell me your daughter's been missing for years?
2	action	continues
1	line	Player	But but but...
1	line	Player	He's not a god! He's a man in a bearsuit! And he killed your daughter!
1	line	Player	And why didn't you tell me she's been missing for more than ten years?
1	line	Hamal the Chieftain	Fourteen years, to be exact. And I didn't say otherwise, did I? As for the Kendal, what proof have you of these serious accusations?
1	cond	If the player hasn't defeated Kendal:
2	line	Player	You want proof for everything, don't you? Why don't you just go and have a look?
2	line	Hamal the Chieftain	Like I said, it is not wise to approach a god so closely.
2	action	end
1	cond	If the player defeated Kendal but isn't wearing the bear mask:
2	line	Player	Well... I've only gone and killed him, haven't I?
1	cond	If the player defeated Kendal and is wearing the bear mask:
2	line	Player	Well... I've only gone and killed him, haven't I? Look, I'm even wearing his silly hat!
1	line	Hamal the Chieftain	You have? Then we made an enormous mistake.
1	line	Hamal the Chieftain	As did he. Impersonating a god bears the highest penalty imaginable, but I did not think that kind of justice would be delivered by the hand of an outerlander.
1	line	Player	I also found your daughter. The bearman killed her.
1	line	Hamal the Chieftain	So it is true then, after all? Let me see her body please.
1	cond	If the player doesn't have the corpse:
2	line	Player	I didn't bring it with me, I'll go and look for it.
2	action	end
1	cond	If the player has the corpse:
2	line	Player	Of course.
2	line	Hamal the Chieftain	Yes, I see it is her. So it is also true that you spoke to her spirit at the pool?
2	line	Player	Yes, it's true.
2	line	Hamal the Chieftain	Then she must be buried there, on the island at the center of the pool... to bring peace to her spirit. This is a most unusual situation, but will you bury her, {player}?
2	option	I will.
3	line	Player	I will.
3	line	Hamal the Chieftain	Then please listen closely. If she is given a proper burial, she is to be buried with some of her possessions. I see she's still wearing her dress, so you will need one more item.
3	line	Hamal the Chieftain	When she has been buried, you must build a cairn on top. It is our tradition to build these cairns in the shape of our longboats that we use when we need to cross the seas. You can use rocks for this purpose, about five or so should be enough.
3	line	Hamal the Chieftain	Only then are the burial rites complete.
3	line	Player	So I need to bury her with one of her possessions, and build a cairn on top made out of rocks. So where do I get another one of her possessions?
3	line	Hamal the Chieftain	Asleif had little possessions in life and none that have survived the years. I do not know where you might find such a thing, but perhaps Ragnar does, out by the pool.
3	action	end
2	option	I won't.
3	line	Player	I won't.
3	line	Player	I... ehm...
3	line	Player	Errr...
3	line	Player	I will.
3	action	above
